An investigator analyzes a small gene sequenced during the Human Genome Project and observes that one strand of the DNA molecule contains 20 adenines (A), 25 guanines (G), 30 cytosines (C), and 22 thymines (T). How many of each base (A, G, C, T) are present in the complete double-stranded DNA molecule?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
In a double-sugar DNA molecule, the two strands are complementary. This means that adenine (A) always matches with thymine (T), and guanine (G) always matches with cytosite (C). 

The investigator observed the structure of one strand, so the other strand will have the same number of A, G, C, and T as the first strand. Therefore, the total number of each base in the double-stranded molecule is:

- **A:** 20 (from strand 1) + 20 (from strand 2) = 40
- **B:** 25 (from strand 1) + 25 (from strand 2) = 50
- **C:** 30 (from strand 1) + 30 (from strand 2) = 60
- **T:** 22 (from strand 1) + 22 (from strand 2) = 44

The total number of each base in the double-stranded DNA molecule is: $\boxed{40 \text{ A, } 50 \text{ G, } 60 \text{ C, } 44 \text{ T}}$.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

