","The lattice energy \( E_L \) of a crystal structure can be calculated using the Born-Lefeman equation:  
\[
E_L = \frac{4\pi}{\varepsilon_0 \varepsilon_r} \frac{Z_1 Z_2 e^2}{r}
\]  
where:  
- \( \varepsilon_0 \) is the vacuum permittivity (\( 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{F/m} \))  
- \( \varepsilon_r \) is the dielectric constant of the crystal (for NaCl, which is similar to CaCl₂, \( \varepsilon_r \approx 1.4 \) for small distances)  
- \( Z_1 \) and \( Z_2 \) are the charges of the ions (for \( \text{Ca}^{2+} \) and \( \text{Cl}^- \), \( Z_1 = 2 \) and \( Z_2 = -1 \))  
- \( e \) is the elementary charge (\( 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \))  
- \( r \) is the distance between the ions (0.231 nm)  

Substituting the given values:  
\[
E_L = \frac{4\pi}{8.854 \times 10^{-12} \times 1.4} \frac{2 \times (-1) \times (1.602 \times 10^{-19})^2}{0.231 \times 10^{-9}}
\]  
\[
E_L = \frac{4\pi}{1.24956 \times 10^{-11}} \frac{-2.563 \times 10^{-36}}{0.231 \times 10^{-9}}
\]  
\[
