     \]
   - The molar mass of sulfur (S) is 32 g/mol, and the molar mass of oxygen (O₂) is 32 g/mol.
   - The molar mass of SO₂ is 32 g/mol (S) + 2 × 16 g/mol (O) = 64 g/mol.
   - The stoichiometric ratio of sulfur to oxygen is 2:1.
   - Therefore, the required mass of sulfur is:
     \[
     \text{Mass of S} = \frac{57.5 \, \text{g} \times 2}{2} = 57.5 \, \text{g}
     \]

