a walks at 10 kmph and 3 hours after his start , b cycles after him at 20 kmph . how far from the start does b catch up with a ?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
1. **Distance covered by a in 3 hours**:  
   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km}
   \]

2. **Relative speed**:  
   Since b cycles at 20 kmph and a runs at 10 kmph, the relative speed of b relative to a is:  
   \[
   \text{Relative speed} = 20 \text{ kmph} - 10 \text{ kmph} = 10 \text{ kmph}
   \]

3. **Time to catch up**:  
   To cover the distance of 30 km at a relative speed of 10 kmph, b will take:  
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance}}{\text{Relative speed}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ kmph}} = 3 \text{ h}
   \]

4. **Distance from start**:  
   Since b cycles at 20 kmph, the distance b covers in 3 hours is:  
   \[
   \text{Distance} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 60 \text{ km}
   \]

   Therefore, b catches up with a at a distance of \(\boxed{60 \text{ km}}\) from the start.
