(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton is given by \( F = qE \), where \( q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \) and \( E = 500 \, \text{N/C} \).  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}.
\]  
The acceleration of the proton is then:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2.
\]  
(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial speed is \( v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s} \). The proton's speed \( v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s} \) is achieved after time \( t \). Using the kinematic equation \( v = v_0 + at \), we solve for \( t \):  
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.28 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}.
\]  
Final answers:  
(a) \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\)  
(b) \(\boxed{7.28 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\)
