","To solve this problem, we need to consider the concept of time dilation in special relativity. Time dilation occurs when an object is moving at a high speed relative to an observer. The time experienced by the moving object is shorter than the time experienced by the observer at rest.

In this problem, we have a spaceship moving at a speed of 0.6c relative to Earth. We are given that the clock in the cockpit shows a time of 3 years when the ship returns to Earth. Since the clock in the cargo bay is on the same spaceship, it will experience the same time dilation as the clock in the cockpit. Therefore, the time measured by the clock in the cargo bay will also be 3 years when the ship returns to Earth.

The time experienced by the clock on Earth will be longer due to time dilation. To calculate the time experienced by the clock on Earth, we can use the following formula for time dilation:

t' = t / sqrt(1 - v^2/c^2)

where t' is the time experienced by the observer at rest (Earth), t is the time experienced by the moving object (spaceship), v is the relative velocity of the moving object (0.6c), and c is the speed of light.

Plugging in the values, we get:

t' = 3 years / sqrt(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
t' = 3 years / sqrt(1 - 0.36)
t' = 3 years / sqrt(0.64)
t' = 3 years / 0.8
t' = 3.75 years

So, the time experienced by the clock on Earth is 3.75 years. Since the clock in the cargo bay is on the same spaceship, it will also experience the same time dilation as the clock in the cockpit. Therefore, the time measured by the clock in the cargo bar will be 3 years when the ship returns to Earth.",relevance,"# Task Description
