","The lifetime of a particle is inversely proportional to its natural width, which is related to the energy fluctuations in the decay process. The Heisenberg uncertainty principle connects the lifetime (\(\tau\)) to the energy uncertainty (\(\Delta E\)) as:

\[
\Delta E \cdot \tau \approx \hbar c
\]

For the Σ⁺ particle, the lifetime \(\tau = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s. The reduced Planck constant \(\hbar \approx 1.055 \times 10^{-34}\) J s and \(c \approx 3 \times 10^8\) m/s. Thus, the energy uncertainty is:

\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar c}{\tau} = \frac{(1.055 \times 10^{-34})(3 \times 10^8)}{2.0 \times 10^{-23}} = 1.58 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J}
\]

Convert this energy uncertainty to MeV (since the rest energy is given in MeV):

\[
\Delta E \approx 1.58 \times 10^{-18} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} \approx 9.8 \text{ MeV}
\]

This means the energy uncertainty in the decay process is approximately 9.8 MeV. This is the natural width of the Σ⁺ particle.

The range of outcomes of measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:

\[
E_{\text{range}} = E_{\text{rest}} \pm \Delta E = 1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 9.8 \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the expected range of outcomes is:

\[
1374.8 \text{ MeV} \leq E_{\text{measured}} \leq 1395.2 \text{ MeV}
\]

